/*!
    \page opcua-client.html
    \title OPC UA Client
    \previouspage Getting Started
    \nextpage OPC UA Server

    The client half of the application connects to an OPC UA server, browses its address
    space, and reads, writes, subscribes to, and plots variable values. Under the hood
    the UI talks to the \l OpcUaManager facade, which delegates the network work to the
    worker-thread \l OpcUaService (see \l {Architecture}).

    \section1 Connecting to a server

    A connection is described by:

    \list
        \li a \b{discovery/endpoint URL}, for example \c {opc.tcp://127.0.0.1:4840};
        \li a \b{security policy} (for example \e None or \e Basic256Sha256 with signing
            and encryption);
        \li an \b{identity}: anonymous, or a username and password.
    \endlist

    Enter these in the connection form and connect. The client performs discovery and
    endpoint selection, then opens a session. Certificate-based security uses the
    application's PKI store (see \l {Configuration}). While connecting and after
    connecting, the state is shown in the status area and logged in the log panel.

    \section1 Browsing the address space

    Once connected, the server's address space is shown as a tree of \e nodes, fetched
    lazily as you expand branches (backed by \l OpcUaModel). Selecting a node shows its
    \e attributes — node id, node class, data type, access level, value, and so on
    (\l AttributesModel).

    Variable nodes whose values are structured (for example CODESYS structures that do
    not publish a full type definition) are decoded by browsing their instance subtree,
    so you see the individual fields rather than an opaque blob.

    \section1 Reading, writing, and subscribing

    \list
        \li \b Read — request the current value of a variable node.
        \li \b Write — send a new value to a writable variable (subject to its access
            level).
        \li \b Subscribe — register interest in a variable so the server pushes value
            changes. Subscribed variables appear in the \e{Data Access} view
            (\l DataAccessModel), which shows live value, status, and timestamp, and can
            be sorted and filtered (\l DataViewFilterModel).
    \endlist

    Monitored variables can be persisted so they are restored on the next connection
    (see \l {Configuration}).

    \section1 Trends

    Selected variables can be plotted over time in the trend panel. The trend keeps a
    bounded history of samples and computes the plot geometry (\l TrendModel); it can be
    paused and resumed.

    \section1 Logging

    Client activity — connection state, errors, and notable operations — is written to
    the log panel and to the application log file. The log file's folder can be opened
    from the UI, which is the starting point for \l {Troubleshooting}.
*/
